msgid "Configure a product and make I/O progress"
msgstr "Configure a product and make I/O progress"

msgid "Start with the [external application tutorial](../tutorials/source-sdk.md). Use the same fixed SDK or checkout for the commands below. Commands run from its source root; product TOML, tasks, buffers and recovery policy stay in the product repository. The resolver reports supported software choices, not electrical verification of a connected board."
msgstr "Start with the [external application tutorial](../tutorials/source-sdk.md). Use the same fixed SDK or checkout for the commands below. Commands run from its source root; product TOML, tasks, buffers and recovery policy stay in the product repository. The resolver reports supported software choices, not electrical verification of a connected board."

msgid "Choose and compare a complete configuration"
msgstr "Choose and compare a complete configuration"

msgid "Inspect choices before authoring a complete assembly. This query requires no guessed backend or clock:"
msgstr "Inspect choices before authoring a complete assembly. This query requires no guessed backend or clock:"

msgid "`init` refuses an existing file. Profiles `empty`, `gpio` and `uart` write visible TOML values and run the normal resolver; nonempty profiles require an explicit binding. A rejected route is not replaced with another one. Read the generated TOML before adopting its baud rate, event capacity, IRQ priority and budgets. For an external Board, both `init` and `list-bindings` accept `--board-package` instead of `--board`. The command resolves that package path from the working directory; `init` writes it relative to the new TOML directory. Moving the whole product tree preserves this relationship. The reviewed Board package remains separate from the product assembly; use the [Board porting guide](board-porting.md) for package contents and provider boundaries."
msgstr "`init` refuses an existing file. Profiles `empty`, `gpio` and `uart` write visible TOML values and run the normal resolver; nonempty profiles require an explicit binding. A rejected route is not replaced with another one. Read the generated TOML before adopting its baud rate, event capacity, IRQ priority and budgets. For an external Board, both `init` and `list-bindings` accept `--board-package` instead of `--board`. The command resolves that package path from the working directory; `init` writes it relative to the new TOML directory. Moving the whole product tree preserves this relationship. The reviewed Board package remains separate from the product assembly; use the [Board porting guide](board-porting.md) for package contents and provider boundaries."

msgid "The explanation lists public faces, modes, clock, resources, memory domains, declared budgets and execution duties. `list-bindings --json` includes `mode_contracts`, resource claims, `fact_sources` and `limitations`. A binding is still `configuration-required`: backend, clock and all selected instances must resolve together. Missing physical PCB facts remain unknown. MCU queries state `not_established_by_configuration`; Native states `not_applicable`."
msgstr "The explanation lists public faces, modes, clock, resources, memory domains, declared budgets and execution duties. `list-bindings --json` includes `mode_contracts`, resource claims, `fact_sources` and `limitations`. A binding is still `configuration-required`: backend, clock and all selected instances must resolve together. Missing physical PCB facts remain unknown. MCU queries state `not_established_by_configuration`; Native states `not_applicable`."

msgid "Each candidate mode lists its own `resource_keys`, `occupied_resources` and `resource_scope`. A polling mode does not claim the alternative DMA route. Board-first queries retain known SoC and Board reservations without inventing an OS owner before a backend is selected. Assembly queries read facts matching the resolved input hashes. Options can add requirements, so this projection does not replace full-assembly admission. Use the [exact support query](../delivery/capabilities.md) to separate configuration acceptance from model, ARM, SDK and physical evidence."
msgstr "Each candidate mode lists its own `resource_keys`, `occupied_resources` and `resource_scope`. A polling mode does not claim the alternative DMA route. Board-first queries retain known SoC and Board reservations without inventing an OS owner before a backend is selected. Assembly queries read facts matching the resolved input hashes. Options can add requirements, so this projection does not replace full-assembly admission. Use the [exact support query](../delivery/capabilities.md) to separate configuration acceptance from model, ARM, SDK and physical evidence."

msgid "After editing a separate next assembly, compare effective changes:"
msgstr "After editing a separate next assembly, compare effective changes:"

msgid "Both assemblies must resolve. The diff reports effective platform, route, mode, endpoint, storage and budget changes; renaming a source file alone is not a behavior change. JSON distinguishes absent fields from explicit `null`. Scripts that previously consumed default JSON from `explain` must now pass `--json`. Editor hints are assistance; the resolver remains the acceptance authority."
msgstr "Both assemblies must resolve. The diff reports effective platform, route, mode, endpoint, storage and budget changes; renaming a source file alone is not a behavior change. JSON distinguishes absent fields from explicit `null`. Scripts that previously consumed default JSON from `explain` must now pass `--json`. Editor hints are assistance; the resolver remains the acceptance authority."

msgid "The diff also groups `impacts` into `factory_ids`, `public_interface`, `transfer_contract`, `execution` and `storage`. Each item identifies its effective path, before/after value and review obligation. Changed or reordered factory IDs may require consumer changes; a new executor or DMA mode needs its lifecycle and storage contract reviewed. These are declared configuration impacts, not measured timing, memory savings or an assertion that a consumer was migrated."
msgstr "The diff also groups `impacts` into `factory_ids`, `public_interface`, `transfer_contract`, `execution` and `storage`. Each item identifies its effective path, before/after value and review obligation. Changed or reordered factory IDs may require consumer changes; a new executor or DMA mode needs its lifecycle and storage contract reviewed. These are declared configuration impacts, not measured timing, memory savings or an assertion that a consumer was migrated."

msgid "A conflict diagnostic identifies the authored file/key and competing resource owners. Correct those inputs, then configure again. Never fix generated C, weaken a DMA reservation or change a clock implicitly to make a build pass. Configuration budgets are declarations; measure the actual linked image before claiming resource savings. See the [assembly reference](../reference/configuration/assembly.md) and [support matrix](../reference/platforms/support.md)."
msgstr "A conflict diagnostic identifies the authored file/key and competing resource owners. Correct those inputs, then configure again. Never fix generated C, weaken a DMA reservation or change a clock implicitly to make a build pass. Configuration budgets are declarations; measure the actual linked image before claiming resource savings. See the [assembly reference](../reference/configuration/assembly.md) and [support matrix](../reference/platforms/support.md)."

msgid "Automation can add `--diagnostics-json` to receive one structured rejection on standard error, with exit code 1; successful standard output keeps its existing format. Human and JSON diagnostics use the same error record. Unknown/missing fields preserve their context and key sets; resource conflicts identify the resource and competing claims, including owner, mode and source. These records locate authored inputs without silently correcting them. CLI argument syntax errors retain argparse diagnostics and exit code 2."
msgstr "Automation can add `--diagnostics-json` to receive one structured rejection on standard error, with exit code 1; successful standard output keeps its existing format. Human and JSON diagnostics use the same error record. Unknown/missing fields preserve their context and key sets; resource conflicts identify the resource and competing claims, including owner, mode and source. These records locate authored inputs without silently correcting them. CLI argument syntax errors retain argparse diagnostics and exit code 2."

msgid "Preview the maintained software plan"
msgstr "Preview the maintained software plan"

msgid "The plan/artifact commands, Settings, wait selection and serial extensions in this iteration require a development checkout containing those changes. They do not modify the published `v0.2.1` archive; installed-SDK support follows actual verification of the corresponding new SDK."
msgstr "The plan/artifact commands, Settings, wait selection and serial extensions in this iteration require a development checkout containing those changes. They do not modify the published `v0.2.1` archive; installed-SDK support follows actual verification of the corresponding new SDK."

msgid "From the fixed development checkout, inspect the existing complete software plan before starting expensive validation:"
msgstr "From the fixed development checkout, inspect the existing complete software plan before starting expensive validation:"

msgid "Choose a new JSON path in an existing parent directory; the future run directory is not created. The preview derives ordered commands, deadlines, selection reasons, checker and preset metadata, and input identities from the maintained plan. It does not execute a process, install tools, use equipment or access the network. With no observation, case counts remain unknown and the operation needs no prior test run. `--preset` filtering is rejected; the complete authored plan remains the selection authority."
msgstr "Choose a new JSON path in an existing parent directory; the future run directory is not created. The preview derives ordered commands, deadlines, selection reasons, checker and preset metadata, and input identities from the maintained plan. It does not execute a process, install tools, use equipment or access the network. With no observation, case counts remain unknown and the operation needs no prior test run. `--preset` filtering is rejected; the complete authored plan remains the selection authority."

msgid "To include previously observed cases, first build the selected Native preset, then explicitly collect case-list evidence with the existing Native producer:"
msgstr "To include previously observed cases, first build the selected Native preset, then explicitly collect case-list evidence with the existing Native producer:"

msgid "`--observe-only` executes each manifest binary's unfiltered case-list process; it does not execute test cases or CTest, and writes no execution-success summary. Prior summaries and snapshots retain unique `before-observe` paths. The normal producer still executes the complete CTest and Google plan, including disabled cases. The same snapshot binds all manifest binaries, their object contexts and a deduplicated conservative superset of current materialized compiler inputs, shared dependencies and linked files. Ninja supplies the metadata through original command receipts; preview itself never launches Ninja. These facts bind current bytes, without proving a fresh compilation or inventing another build graph. See the [development tool contract](https://github.com/X-Gen-Lab/nexus/blob/NEXUS-DOCUMENTATION-SOURCE/tools/dev/README.md) for bounds and exact receipt requirements."
msgstr "`--observe-only` executes each manifest binary's unfiltered case-list process; it does not execute test cases or CTest, and writes no execution-success summary. Prior summaries and snapshots retain unique `before-observe` paths. The normal producer still executes the complete CTest and Google plan, including disabled cases. The same snapshot binds all manifest binaries, their object contexts and a deduplicated conservative superset of current materialized compiler inputs, shared dependencies and linked files. Ninja supplies the metadata through original command receipts; preview itself never launches Ninja. These facts bind current bytes, without proving a fresh compilation or inventing another build graph. See the [development tool contract](https://github.com/X-Gen-Lab/nexus/blob/NEXUS-DOCUMENTATION-SOURCE/tools/dev/README.md) for bounds and exact receipt requirements."

msgid "Preview accepts an explicit observation only while its source, objects, headers, archives, binaries, metadata and successful original discovery receipts still match. Receipts must belong to this build and follow its snapshot. Stale, failed, changed or incomplete observations reject; a relocated checkout or SDK needs a new observation. Parameterized and disabled identities are preserved. Cases present in the reviewed lifecycle catalogue show its invariant and limitations; unreviewed cases remain `unknown`, without a coverage percentage. The displayed result means “previously observed and current inputs match”; it never means these cases executed during preview or passed."
msgstr "Preview accepts an explicit observation only while its source, objects, headers, archives, binaries, metadata and successful original discovery receipts still match. Receipts must belong to this build and follow its snapshot. Stale, failed, changed or incomplete observations reject; a relocated checkout or SDK needs a new observation. Parameterized and disabled identities are preserved. Cases present in the reviewed lifecycle catalogue show its invariant and limitations; unreviewed cases remain `unknown`, without a coverage percentage. The displayed result means “previously observed and current inputs match”; it never means these cases executed during preview or passed."

msgid "Known host stages need no equipment. A HIL preparation stage can show declared fixture tests and pending measurements, but missing resolved configuration stays `configuration_pending`; routes and station facts are not guessed. Unknown hardware needs stay `unknown`, and physical status stays `not_executed`. Every preview stage remains `not_executed` and qualification remains false. Remove only your own preview JSON when no longer needed."
msgstr "Known host stages need no equipment. A HIL preparation stage can show declared fixture tests and pending measurements, but missing resolved configuration stays `configuration_pending`; routes and station facts are not guessed. Unknown hardware needs stay `unknown`, and physical status stays `not_executed`. Every preview stage remains `not_executed` and qualification remains false. Remove only your own preview JSON when no longer needed."

msgid "After a successful build, inspect its captured outputs without guessing which ELF belongs to the selected configuration. Replace the path below with that build's actual `quality/build-provenance.json`:"
msgstr "After a successful build, inspect its captured outputs without guessing which ELF belongs to the selected configuration. Replace the path below with that build's actual `quality/build-provenance.json`:"

msgid "The command verifies the provenance and resolved identity before listing source, configuration, artifact roles and the original command receipt. Missing, changed, stale or mismatched inputs reject rather than becoming a successful artifact view. The optional JSON path must be new. Inspection neither builds nor accesses equipment, and does not grant the current checkout, software or hardware a new qualification."
msgstr "The command verifies the provenance and resolved identity before listing source, configuration, artifact roles and the original command receipt. Missing, changed, stale or mismatched inputs reject rather than becoming a successful artifact view. The optional JSON path must be new. Inspection neither builds nor accesses equipment, and does not grant the current checkout, software or hardware a new qualification."

msgid "Use optional Settings and networking mechanisms"
msgstr "Use optional Settings and networking mechanisms"

msgid "Select `settings` in the assembly's component list to link `Nexus::Settings`. The caller first opens Storage, then supplies Settings metadata, a disjoint staging buffer, entry capacity and a product-defined schema. Integer keys and copied byte values stay bounded. Get/set/remove operate on staging; only explicit commit writes Storage, and close discards uncommitted changes. Unknown schemas or corrupt loads are errors, not empty writable stores. After a mutating commit failure, reopen Storage and Settings to observe the surviving commit before retrying. Defaults, migration, authorization and Flash regions remain product policy. Read {ref}`settings.h <settings_8h>` and the [component contract](https://github.com/X-Gen-Lab/nexus/blob/NEXUS-DOCUMENTATION-SOURCE/components/settings/README.md) for detailed ownership."
msgstr "Select `settings` in the assembly's component list to link `Nexus::Settings`. The caller first opens Storage, then supplies Settings metadata, a disjoint staging buffer, entry capacity and a product-defined schema. Integer keys and copied byte values stay bounded. Get/set/remove operate on staging; only explicit commit writes Storage, and close discards uncommitted changes. Unknown schemas or corrupt loads are errors, not empty writable stores. After a mutating commit failure, reopen Storage and Settings to observe the surviving commit before retrying. Defaults, migration, authorization and Flash regions remain product policy. Read {ref}`settings.h <settings_8h>` and the [component contract](https://github.com/X-Gen-Lab/nexus/blob/NEXUS-DOCUMENTATION-SOURCE/components/settings/README.md) for detailed ownership."

msgid "The optional `Nexus::LwIP` target admits the organization-hosted `X-Gen-Lab/lwip` 2.2.1 gitlink `77dcd25a` and its fixed source closure. It is excluded from the default build until explicitly selected. It uses `NO_SYS=1`: a product loop or task advances one serialized owner, without an implicit RTOS stack port. It reuses Ethernet frame loans and bounded memory. The reviewed slice covers static IPv4/ARP/ICMP/UDP and limited TCP integration; complete TCP connection lifecycles, DHCP, DNS, IPv6, VLAN, TLS and MQTT remain separate exits. Read the [network integration contract](https://github.com/X-Gen-Lab/nexus/blob/NEXUS-DOCUMENTATION-SOURCE/components/network/README.md) for source, SDK, clock, memory and stop prerequisites. Source admission and development SDK consumption do not establish a software candidate, full network-platform, ARM or physical acceptance."
msgstr "The optional `Nexus::LwIP` target admits the organization-hosted `X-Gen-Lab/lwip` 2.2.1 gitlink `77dcd25a` and its fixed source closure. It is excluded from the default build until explicitly selected. It uses `NO_SYS=1`: a product loop or task advances one serialized owner, without an implicit RTOS stack port. It reuses Ethernet frame loans and bounded memory. The reviewed slice covers static IPv4/ARP/ICMP/UDP and limited TCP integration; complete TCP connection lifecycles, DHCP, DNS, IPv6, VLAN, TLS and MQTT remain separate exits. Read the [network integration contract](https://github.com/X-Gen-Lab/nexus/blob/NEXUS-DOCUMENTATION-SOURCE/components/network/README.md) for source, SDK, clock, memory and stop prerequisites. Source admission and development SDK consumption do not establish a software candidate, full network-platform, ARM or physical acceptance."

msgid "Assign one executor and retain borrowed storage"
msgstr "Assign one executor and retain borrowed storage"

